    {
      "id": "section-01",
      "heading": "DISLOCATION OF KNEE CAP",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "assets/images/articles/2dcfdc2cc923ad98e46e.jpg",
          "alt": "Knee X-ray",
          "lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "What Is Knee Cap Dislocation ?",
          "question": true
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The knee cap could dislocate to the outer side of the knee. This is commonly seen in children with lax joints. Many a time it comes back to normal position automatically."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "assets/images/articles/732051f145dea2d6bda7.jpg",
          "alt": "Knee support brace with an opening over the kneecap",
          "lang": "en"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "What Is The Treatment For This ?",
          "question": true
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Using a knee cap stabilizing knee support for 1-2 weeks will help these children. In some children this recurs. They need investigations to see if there are abnormalities of the knee that cannot keep the kneecap in place."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
